Revision history of WwW-SiteConf


0.01_04
    + siteconf -i preset improvement!
      For the Web preset you can suppress render template output
      with -o render:/dev/null.  You can specify a different
      name in general, e.g. say you organize utilities in a
      directory, then you can use -o render:gen/render.

      The render template will adapt to the name you choose,
      so it would read `# HAVE: gen/render' in the header.

      !!! Note that this file is (still) generated relative to
          the project root, and (still) needs a sane name since
          it needs to be accounted for in the dep graph.  I *try*
          to be forgiving with this, but in general keep it ASCII,
          URL-safe and free of whitespace (ESPECIALLY \x{20} and
          any C0 controls).

      *** The template name is omissible and defaults to configure
          UNLESS your target file contains a colon, in which case
          you must now write `-o configure:weird:path'.

      *** siteconf -i also no longer clobbers configure by default.
          You must pass -f, or (in a script) pass -q and read the
          exit status to see if configure was ever updated.

    * general configure manual: RdKw::WwW::SiteConf::Configure.
      Written by GPT-6 based on man/configure.pod from cooper-www.

    *** A file named `render' in the project directory no longer
        depends on ${B}/MY/option.mk implicitly.  This was already
        the case in www-cellar 87aa96e7ace3f96d1595ad26b1b71a175f746abe
        (Un-bake MY/option.mk as a dependency of render, on Aug 21)
        so i'm not sure where Claude got that from when it generated
        commit 0f20f3f31c51af9d0725a0e12c6c6c3f4fb12a67 (Oct 30)....
        It is "breaking" nonetheless if you had an older render script
        but I think it is for the better. :v

    + Prerequisites of a rule (the renderer or anything outside
      ${a} in general*) may join the dep graph too!  Their NEEDs
      and WANTs are also parsed.  (This is how the Web preset's
      render still depends on ${B}/MY/option.mk, in spite of the
      removal of special-casing above.)
          * any prerequisite that `siteconf' does not build
            is processed; they don't have to be outside ${a}
            specifically.  Thanks Claude for the correction

        *** The HAVE line is still checked for consistency;
            this was previously ignored so you have to fix it
            for any script generated like -o render:other-path
            (or perhaps remove the line, but well... your choice.)

    + ${A} is no longer special.  You can now ./configure -DA=...
      to use a different project root (though that project should
      contain all the files you NEED in your MANIFEST, obviously!)

      * An absolute A is kept as is in MY/config.mk, like B is.
        Only a *relative* A remains computed as a path relative
        to the build directory.  This is so that people can use
        absolute paths to certify that it is safe and probably
        shorter for cd -- ${A:Q} too.

    *** ./configure no longer resolves -DA relative to itself.
        You must change into to the same directory or override
        -DA; i.e., do `cd /my/proj && ./configure' or
        `/my/proj/configure -DA=/my/proj'.  (A nice way to
        convince yourself is to see if configure could still
        be run if you run `perl -</path/to/configure'; it
        wouldn't (and frankly should not) know where it is. :)


0.01_03  2026-11-01  RC
    Template update. :[

    * don't clobber %O with location
    * more transparent render script

0.01_02  2026-11-01  RC
    * Makefile.in target and dependency lists support backslash
      continuations across multiple lines.
    * BUG fix: -Db=. is now supported (though with degraded
      diagnostic messages, it's better than completely broken)

0.01_01  2026-10-31  RC
    * siteconf -g can now -o-utput to a file different from the
      backend default (such as Makefile.proj instead of Makefile).
      It can also override manifest and ruleset with -F and -R.
    *** the manifest (F) and the rules (R) are now paths from
      the current directory, the same for -F, -R and the configured
      -DF, -DR; they used to be relative to the project root (A).
      They are not part of the source tree.  To keep the old meaning,
      write the path from where you run `siteconf -g' (e.g. ../MANIFEST).
      (But I mean, the old meaning wasn't even intentional; so...)
    * everyone understands -q now!
    * siteconf -g tooltip (similar to what ./configure does)
    * siteconf -i now writes the options it received (similar to
      how BIND 9 dig +cmd prints the options at the top).  should
      be helpful to folks who need to re-generate it over updates.


0.01_00  2026-10-31  RC
    this is mostly a clean-up of AI-generated oddities
    i didn't like, but nonetheless breaking so i bumped
    the first two digits.

    + non-web-specific logic has been extracted to GenGenLib.
      (we do not depend on GenLib.)

    + -V for printing versions.  double -V for library versions
      (possibly useful for bug hunting)

    *** the web preset now generates stock render-er.  it does
      not generate the Makefile.in that makes it useful though.

    + patch-friendly siteconf -i -D and -O help text


0.00_00  2026-10-31  RC
    !>! written with substantial help from Anthropic Claude !<!

    first release!

    * siteconf -i (init) writes ./configure from a template and
      a preset (-P web).  This ./configure script has been based
      on www-cellar (which was written by me, at least the core
      of it), which was a re-design of the cooper-www experiment.

      The text is deterministic, so local changes may be
      kept as a patch on top of it.

    * ./configure writes MY/config.mk (definitions, the D
      namespace) and MY/option.mk (options, the O namespace).

    * siteconf -g (make) reads those and writes the build file,
      -G make (Makefile) or -G ninja (build.ninja), and MY/
      MANIFEST.<goal> for each goal (aka phony target).

    * MANIFEST is MAKE/NEED/WANT.  A WANT is resolved at
      configuration time: dropped, with a warning, unless
      it can be had.

    * Headers (HAVE/NEED/WANT) name paths from the project
      root: ${A}/${a}/index.html, ${B}/${b}/style.css, ${B}/
      MY/option.mk.  Depending on something in ${B}/${b} is
      an edge to that file, which is made first (cycles are
      an error).
